WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
Which cells in the retina carry a gene's message?

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.380
The retina is the layered tissue lining the back of the eye.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.860
The cells are still in their places, but their messages are too small to see.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.860
We need a way to mark where a particular message is.

5
00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.137
Zoom into a cell to follow the message.

6
00:00:21.457 --> 00:00:23.197
A cell can make a message from a gene.

7
00:00:24.597 --> 00:00:27.057
Making that message is part of gene expression.

8
00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:30.437
The message is called messenger RNA.

9
00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:34.957
Here, we want to find the cells where that message is present.

10
00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:40.310
To find the message, experimenters add a matching piece called a probe.

11
00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.450
The probe carries a label so we can see where it stays.

12
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:50.850
When it meets the matching RNA, the two bind, and unbound probes are washed away.

13
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:56.033
That pairing is called hybridization, and in situ means in place.

14
00:00:57.433 --> 00:01:02.633
Together, in situ hybridization marks RNA while cells stay in place in the tissue.

15
00:01:03.953 --> 00:01:08.053
As we zoom back out, the marks give us a map of where the message is present.

16
00:01:09.117 --> 00:01:11.377
Let's use that rule to read the retina.

17
00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:16.377
Imagine the message is present only in cells in the inner layers.

18
00:01:17.717 --> 00:01:19.677
Picture the probe meeting its match there.

19
00:01:20.877 --> 00:01:24.817
When you zoom out and look at the whole section, where would you expect the marks to

20
00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:25.817
appear?

21
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:35.050
The marks would appear in the inner layers, because that is where the probe has a

22
00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:39.850
match. Now imagine looking for the same message with the wrong probe.

23
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.650
You could miss the message even in cells where it really is present.

24
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:49.153
So check that the probe matches the message you mean to find.

25
00:01:50.513 --> 00:01:53.473
Even with the right probe, the map shows only location.

26
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:58.873
Having an RNA message alone does not make a cell light-sensitive.

27
00:01:59.773 --> 00:02:03.953
Testing light responses takes a separate recording in living cells.

28
00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:11.147
In 2000, Provencio and colleagues used this method to map a gene called melanopsin.

29
00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:17.367
They tested retinal tissue from adult macaque and from adult and 10-day-old mice.

30
00:02:19.087 --> 00:02:23.667
Marks appeared in the inner retina, in the ganglion and amacrine cell layers, with

31
00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:25.247
no light response recorded.

32
00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:31.210
In 2019, Peng and colleagues profiled cells from adult macaque retinas.

33
00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.450
To check which cells each group matched, they used fluorescent in situ

34
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:42.170
hybridization. Fluorescent means the probe's label gives off light, making marks

35
00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:43.170
visible.

36
00:02:43.633 --> 00:02:47.333
Peng and colleagues also used a virus to label cell shapes.

37
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:52.993
Together, the fluorescent marks and shapes checked the identities of two ganglion

38
00:02:52.993 --> 00:02:55.593
cell groups, called midget and parasol.
